Register the topologies appended to the packet, like the Pixel 3a

This undoes a two-day dead end, and it undoes it because the code of the only
place where this really works has finally been looked at.

## What was believed

That `AVCS_CMD_REGISTER_TOPOLOGIES` needs shared memory. The deduction came
from the fact that the first attempt -sending the block appended to the
packet- got `ADSP_EBADPARAM`. From there everything was rewritten to map a
region with `dma_alloc_coherent`, and that path ends up **rebooting the whole
phone**.

## What the Pixel 3a actually does

`sdm670-mainline/linux-patches`, file
`ASoC-qdsp6-q6core-add-support-for-registering-topologies.patch`. It is the
only place in mainline where topology registration works, on the sibling SoC of
ours. And it **sends the block appended to the packet. It never maps memory at
any point.**

Its structure has the address fields, but leaves them at zero on purpose:

    p = kzalloc(pkt_size, GFP_KERNEL);      /* leaves everything at zero */
    ...
    cmd->payload_size = size;               /* the ONLY field it fills in */
    memcpy(dest, data, size);

`payload_addr_lsw`, `payload_addr_msw` and `mem_map_handle` at zero **is** the
"the data comes inside the packet" mechanism. It is not an oversight.

It was also checked that in its whole patch tree
`AVCS_CMD_SHARED_MEM_MAP_REGIONS` does not appear even once, nor `dma_alloc`,
nor any device tree property for memory. Only `qcom,acdb-name`, which we
already have.

## So why the EBADPARAM

For a much simpler reason, found the same day:

    what the kernel was reading   485b0439...   <- Global_cal.acdb of the PIXEL 4a
    what is really this phone's   72b7f3fd...   <- it was only in the factory partition

The DSP was being asked to register **another phone's topologies**. And not
because of a one-off slip: the file the project packaged *as if it were*
surya's is also a byte-for-byte copy of the Pixel 4a's. There was no way to get
it right until the genuine one was pulled out of the factory partition (see
`docs/STOCK-REFERENCE.md`).

"Invalid parameter" is exactly the right answer to that.

## What this patch does

  - It goes back to sending the block appended to the packet, copying the Pixel
    3a literally, and makes it **the default**.
  - It leaves the shared memory path behind a parameter, documented as
    dangerous, so as not to lose the work:

        /sys/module/q6core/parameters/register_through_memory    0 = appended [default]
                                                              1 = memory (REBOOTS THE PHONE)

  - It traces the size of the block and of the packet, so as to see at a glance
    whether the file being read is the right one: with the Pixel 4a's, the
    block was 1316 bytes.

The ACDB parsing and the offset (`+4`, skipping the length prefix) already
matched the Pixel 3a's field by field; that is not touched.

## How to test it, without spending a call

    echo 1 > /sys/bus/aprbus/devices/aprsvc:service:4:3/register_topologies
    dmesg | tail

The DSP accepts or rejects. If it accepts, then -and only then- does it make
sense to put in the topology identifiers from surya's calibration and try it in
a call.

--- a/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6core.c
+++ b/sound/soc/qcom/qdsp6/q6core.c
@@ -414,7 +414,90 @@
 	core->mem_map_handle = 0;
 }
 
-static int q6core_register_topologies(struct device *dev, u32 size, const void *data)
+/*
+ * Delivery method for the topology block:
+ *
+ *   0 = ATTACHED TO THE APR PACKET (in-band).  [default]
+ *   1 = via shared memory.                     DANGER: takes the phone down.
+ *
+ *   /sys/module/q6core/parameters/register_through_memory
+ *
+ * WHY THE DEFAULT CHANGES TO IN-BAND
+ * -----------------------------------
+ * The Pixel 3a patch (sdm670-mainline/linux-patches,
+ * ASoC-qdsp6-q6core-add-support-for-registering-topologies.patch), which is the
+ * only place where this really works in mainline, **sends the block
+ * attached to the packet and never maps memory at any point**. Its struct
+ * carries payload_addr_lsw/msw and mem_map_handle, but leaves them at ZERO on
+ * purpose: it allocates the packet with kzalloc and only fills in payload_size.
+ *
+ * Here it was done the other way round. The first attempt was in-band, the DSP answered
+ * ADSP_EBADPARAM, and it was inferred that shared memory was needed. That
+ * inference led to a dead end: mapping the region **reboots the
+ * whole phone**.
+ *
+ * The EBADPARAM had a much simpler explanation. The DSP was being
+ * given the calibration file of the **Pixel 4a**: the one the kernel read
+ * (md5 485b0439...) is sunfish's Global_cal.acdb, and the one the project
+ * packaged "from surya" is also a byte for byte copy of the same one. The
+ * real one for this phone (md5 72b7f3fd...) only existed inside the factory
+ * partition, and was extracted on 2026-08-11 (see docs/STOCK-REFERENCE.md).
+ *
+ * That is: the DSP was being asked to register another phone's topologies.
+ * "Bad parameter" is exactly the right answer to that.
+ */
+static unsigned int register_through_memory;
+module_param(register_through_memory, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(register_through_memory,
+		 "0 = inline in the packet (like the Pixel 3a), 1 = shared memory (DANGER: reboots the phone)");
+
+/* The way the Pixel 3a patch does it: the block goes inside the packet. */
+static int q6core_register_topologies_inband(struct device *dev, u32 size,
+					     const void *data)
+{
+	struct q6core *core = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	struct avcs_cmd_register_topologies *cmd;
+	struct apr_pkt *pkt;
+	void *p, *dest;
+	int pkt_size, rc;
+
+	pkt_size = APR_HDR_SIZE + sizeof(*cmd) + size;
+
+	p = kzalloc(pkt_size, GFP_KERNEL);
+	if (!p)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
+	pkt = p;
+	cmd = p + APR_HDR_SIZE;
+	dest = p + APR_HDR_SIZE + sizeof(*cmd);
+
+	pkt->hdr.hdr_field = APR_HDR_FIELD(APR_MSG_TYPE_SEQ_CMD,
+					   APR_HDR_LEN(APR_HDR_SIZE), APR_PKT_VER);
+	pkt->hdr.pkt_size = pkt_size;
+	pkt->hdr.opcode = AVCS_CMD_REGISTER_TOPOLOGIES;
+
+	/*
+	 * kzalloc leaves payload_addr_lsw, payload_addr_msw and mem_map_handle at
+	 * zero, and THAT is what tells the DSP that the data comes inside the
+	 * packet. It is not an oversight, it is the mechanism.
+	 */
+	cmd->payload_size = size;
+	memcpy(dest, data, size);
+
+	dev_info(dev, "in-band registration: %u bytes of topologies, packet of %d\n",
+		 size, pkt_size);
+
+	core->resp_received = false;
+	rc = apr_send_pkt(core->adev, pkt);
+	kfree(pkt);
+	if (rc >= 0)
+		q6core_wait_resp(core);
+
+	return rc < 0 ? rc : 0;
+}
+
+static int q6core_register_topologies_shmem(struct device *dev, u32 size,
+					    const void *data)
 {
 	struct q6core *core = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
 	struct avcs_cmd_register_topologies *cmd;
@@ -487,6 +570,15 @@
 	return rc < 0 ? rc : 0;
 }
 
+static int q6core_register_topologies(struct device *dev, u32 size,
+				      const void *data)
+{
+	if (register_through_memory)
+		return q6core_register_topologies_shmem(dev, size, data);
+
+	return q6core_register_topologies_inband(dev, size, data);
+}
+
